  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary:\nA low-privileged participant can read another participant's live Talk `sessionId` from the room participant list, then use moderator rights in an unrelated room they control to inject moderator-only internal-signaling `control` messages into the victim's room session.\n\nThe server checks moderator permissions only against the sender session's own room, but it queues the message to any attacker-supplied recipient `sessionId`. Message delivery is then keyed only by recipient session ID, not by room. I reproduced this by sending a `forceMute` control from room B into a victim active in room A.\n\nWhen the victim is in a call, the shipped client handles `forceMute` by muting the local user session.\n\n## Product / Version\n\n- Nextcloud Server: `34.0.0.1` (`status.php` reported `versionstring: 34.0.0 dev`)\n- Talk app: `24.0.0-dev.2`\n- `spreed` commit: `031e0708bde280b2a83943a0e7a6102d8eede207`\n- Server commit used in the lab: `b71661a38bb40fbdfc071f273ea506a4a9fae091`\n- Reproduced with the default internal-signaling mode on a local Nextcloud lab\n\n## Affected Code\n\n- `lib/Controller/RoomController.php:1217-1220`\n  Active participant session IDs are exposed in the participant list.\n- `lib/Controller/RoomController.php:1250-1253`\n  Single-session participants are returned with `sessionIds`.\n- `lib/Controller/SignalingController.php:387-418`\n  `control` messages are authorized against the sender's session, then queued to arbitrary `decodedMessage['to']`.\n- `lib/Signaling/Messages.php:48-56`\n  Queue entries store `sender`, `recipient`, and `message` only.\n- `lib/Signaling/Messages.php:102-110`\n  Messages are fetched solely by `recipient = $sessionId`.\n- `lib/Controller/SignalingController.php:551-585`\n  Victim polling consumes any queued message for that session.\n- `src/utils/webrtc/simplewebrtc/simplewebrtc.js:108-113`\n  Incoming `forceMute` causes the client to mute itself when `peerId` matches the current session.\n\n## Steps To Reproduce:\n\nPreconditions:\n- Victim and attacker both participate in room A.\n- Attacker is only a normal participant in room A.\n- Attacker owns or moderates unrelated room B.\n- Internal signaling is enabled.\n\nSteps:\n\n  1. On nextcloud  spreed (34.0.0.1 / Talk 24.0.0-dev.2), `victim` created room A and added `attacker` (evil). Both joined room A.\n     evil queried GET /ocs/v2.php/apps/spreed/api/v4/room/97oqymwq/participants?format=json and received victim’s live room-A session ID JRal9O65....\n  2. evil created unrelated room B, joined it, and got room-B sender session pekgCNiV....\n  3. evil sent this to room B:\n\n     {\n       \"messages\": \"[{\\\"ev\\\":\\\"message\\\",\\\"sessionId\\\":\\\"<evil_room_b_session>\\\",\\\"fn\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"control\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"to\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"<victim_room_a_session>\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"payload\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"action\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"forceMute\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"peerId\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"<victim_room_a_session>\\\\\\\"}}\\\"}]\"\n     }\n     to POST /ocs/v2.php/apps/spreed/api/v3/signaling/pfo7kdcz?format=json\n  4. Server-side evidence showed a queued row with sender pekgCNiV... and recipient JRal9O65... in oc_talk_internalsignaling.\n  5. victim then polled room A with GET /ocs/v2.php/apps/spreed/api/v3/signaling/97oqymwq?format=json and received the injected control message with \"from\":\"pekgCNiV...\".\n  6. Negative control: sending the same forceMute from room A itself, using evil’s room-A session QBL3zUuH..., created no victim-targeted queue row and victim’s next poll returned only usersInRoom.\n\n\n## Supporting Material/References:\n\nHere the attacker reads the session of the victim in the shared room\n{F5634941}\n\nthe attacker uses the victim's session as the recipient and the attackers room B as the sender\n{F5634940}\n\nvictim in room A receives injected message from attacker in room B and is muted \n{F5634942}\n\n## Impact\n\n- A low-privileged user can inject moderator-only internal-signaling controls into a room they do not moderate.\n- The reproduced payload is `forceMute`. When the victim is in a call, the shipped client handles this by muting the local session.\n- More generally, the room boundary for internal signaling is broken because delivery is keyed only by recipient session ID.",
